From 7367daeb2ff4c52b68dbcc57d08df0b659f54882 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Golle <daniel@makrotopia.org>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 14:25:36 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] state: narrate the shutdown on /dev/kmsg

From STATE_INIT onwards procd logs to syslog, so its narration of the
shutdown has to reach logd over a datagram socket and be written out by
logread before STATE_HALT's kill(-1, SIGTERM) destroys both. Only
"- shutdown -" wins that race, by luck of the inittab run and the sync()
that follow it; "- SIGTERM processes -" loses although it is emitted
before the kill it announces, and the later markers are logged when logd
is already dead. Reordering cannot help, because logd has to die before
the machine does.

Switch the channel to /dev/kmsg on entry to STATE_SHUTDOWN, symmetric to
the switch to syslog at STATE_INIT. The kernel owns that ring to the
last instant and echoes it to the console, and logd reads /proc/kmsg, so
the markers still reach logread while logd lives; the switch precedes
set_console() so that its own failures are reported too. Syslog is not
kept in parallel, as it would only duplicate the one marker that already
survives. The lines keep their procd: prefix but move from facility
daemon to user, which the kernel forces on anything userspace writes to
/dev/kmsg.

Fixes: 4bccbfa967d7 ("Convert log calls to ulog() api")
Signed-off-by: Daniel Golle <daniel@makrotopia.org>
(cherry picked from commit 2e9b11af58f6b930fabd41c6339428e9fe28b91a)
---
 state.c | 1 +
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)

diff --git a/state.c b/state.c
index 478919c..957afce 100644
--- a/state.c
+++ b/state.c
@@ -171,6 +171,7 @@ static void state_enter(void)
 		break;
 
 	case STATE_SHUTDOWN:
+		ulog_open(ULOG_KMSG, LOG_DAEMON, "procd");
 		/* Redirect output to the console for the users' benefit */
 		set_console();
 		LOG("- shutdown -\n");
